<title>CROATIA: Croatia to import 40-80,000 T wheat to bridge gap.</title>
<headline>Croatia to import 40-80,000 T wheat to bridge gap.</headline>
<dateline>ZAGREB 1997-01-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Croatia will have to import 40,000 to 80,000 tonnes of wheat to bridge a consumption gap until this year's harvest, the agriculture ministry said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;Sowings were lower and the crop turned out weaker than expected (in 1996),&quot; spokesman Zeljko Brkic told Reuters.</p>
<p>He put domestic demand at some 750,000 tonnes a year.</p>
<p>This year's crop from some 300,000 hectares sown to wheat is estimated to total between 950,000 and 1.1 million tonnes.</p>
<p>&quot;With a good crop we should have enough for both our needs and for export,&quot; Brkic said.</p>
<p>Croatia used to export some 400,000 tonnes of wheat annually to neighbouring Slovenia and Bosnia before the 1991 war that split up former Yugoslavia.</p>
<p>--Metka Jelenc, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-4557075</p>
</text>
